==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|monstrous}}, from {{der|en|fro|monstrueuse}}, {{m|fro|monstrüos}}, from {{der|en|la|monstrosus|mōnstrōsus}}. Compare {{m|en|monstruous}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɑnstɹəs/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈmɒnstɹəs/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-monstrous.wav|Audio (UK)}}
* {{hyph|en|mon|strous}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[hideous|Hideous]] or [[frightful]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=III|scene=iii|passage=So bad a death argues a '''monstrous''' life.}}
# [[enormously|Enormously]] [[large]].
#: ''a '''monstrous''' height''
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 20, 1901|url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/bibliography/69252|journal=The Agricultural Journal and Mining Record|volume=4|issue=21|page=663|title=The Ringing of Plants|passage=Possibly monster pumpkins may become still more '''monstrous''' by the shoots being ringed, and so may other vegetables and fruits where quality is of less importance than mere size.}}
# [[freakish|Freakish]] or [[grotesque]].
#* {{RQ:Locke Human Understanding|3}}
#*: The irregular and '''monstrous''' births
#* '''1650''', {{w|Jeremy Taylor}}, ''The rule and exercises of holy living''
#*: He, therefore, that refuses to do good to them whom he is bound to love {{...}} is unnatural and '''monstrous''' in his affections.
# Of, or relating to a [[mythical]] [[monster]]; full of monsters.
#* {{RQ:Milton Poems|poem=Lycidas|passage=Where thou, perhaps, under the whelming tide / Visitest the bottom of the '''monstrous''' world.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[marvellous|Marvellous]]; exceedingly [[strange]]; [[fantastical]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1887|author=H. Rider Haggard|authorlink=H. Rider Haggard|title=She: A History of Adventure|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/3155/3155-0.txt|passage=The whole story was '''monstrous''', and only worthy of the superstitious days in which it was written.}}

